    "The first Greeks had reached Australia already in the 19th century, many from Kythira, Kastellorizo and Ithaca. But the great emigration came after the Second World War. Australia wanted workers and population (“populate or perish” was the slogan), and in 1952 it signed an agreement with Greece for assisted migration. Until the early 1970s thousands of young people a year sailed for four or five weeks on ships such as the *Patris* to work in factories, on dams such as the Snowy Mountains scheme, and on railways. Young women came too, many as domestic workers or as brides for men already there.",
